course: dictionary
lesson: 3388
title: Expansion: growing bigger
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Expansion is a noun, and it comes from the verb expand. It means getting bigger in size, amount or space. «The expansion of the town meant new houses on the hill.» Roots ending in d, de, se or nd usually take -sion, so expand becomes expansion.
explain: In science, expansion means that heat makes things take up more space. «Bridges grow longer in heat and shorter in cold.» Engineers know this, so they build gaps and joints into bridges and railway tracks.
explain: In maths, expansion can mean writing a number in a longer form, as a sum of its place values. For example, 345 = 300 + 40 + 5. Each part shows the value of one digit.
fact: Expansion means getting bigger in size, amount or space.
fact: Thermal expansion is when heat makes a material take up more space.
fact: «Expansion joints» are metal teeth or gaps that let a bridge move without cracking.
fact: «Long bridges can change length by tens of centimetres between winter and summer.»
fact: In maths, 345 written as 300 + 40 + 5 is in expanded form.
exercise: Which word from the lesson means getting bigger in size, amount or space? | Tension | Shrinkage | *Expansion | Division
exercise: What do expansion joints let a bridge do? | Stay completely still forever | *Move when it grows or shrinks | Hold up more cars | Get shorter in the heat
exercise: In maths, how is 345 written in expanded form? | 3 + 4 + 5 | *300 + 40 + 5 | 345 + 0 | 34 × 5
test: Roots ending in d, de, se or nd usually take which ending to make a noun like expansion? | -ly | -ness | -ing | *-sion
test: What does «thermal expansion» mean? | Cold makes things grow bigger | *Heat makes a material take up more space | Writing a number in words | A business growing into new markets
exam: Why do bridges and railway tracks have gaps, linked to expansion? | To make them lighter to carry | Trains need room to turn around | To let water drain away quickly | *So they can grow in heat without cracking or buckling
